---
import DocsLayout from '../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import MermaidDiagram from '../components/MermaidDiagram.astro';
import {
  systemDiagram,
  agentInteractionDiagram,
  hooksDiagram,
  commandsDiagram,
  scriptsDiagram,
  runtimeStateDiagram,
  safetyDiagram,
  dashboardDiagram,
} from '../data/diagrams';
import { commands, categories } from '../data/commands';
---

<DocsLayout title="How It Works" sectionLabel="Architecture" activeSlug="arch-visual">

  <section class="section">
    <h2>How VibeCrew Works</h2>
    <p>VibeCrew is a system of interlocking parts: a plugin shell, specialized agents, lifecycle hooks, slash commands, bash scripts, and per-project runtime state. Select a tab to see how each part fits together.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- Tab navigation -->
  <div class="arch-tabs" role="tablist" aria-label="Architecture sections">
    <button class="arch-tab active" role="tab" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="panel-plugin" data-tab="plugin">Plugin Structure</button>
    <button class="arch-tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="panel-agents" data-tab="agents">Sub-Agents</button>
    <button class="arch-tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="panel-hooks" data-tab="hooks">Hooks</button>
    <button class="arch-tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="panel-commands" data-tab="commands">Slash Commands</button>
    <button class="arch-tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="panel-skills" data-tab="skills">Skills Reference</button>
    <button class="arch-tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="panel-scripts" data-tab="scripts">Bash Scripts</button>
    <button class="arch-tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="panel-runtime" data-tab="runtime">Runtime State</button>
    <button class="arch-tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="panel-safety" data-tab="safety">Permissions &amp; Safety</button>
    <button class="arch-tab" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="panel-dashboard" data-tab="dashboard">Vibe Dashboard</button>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════ Plugin Structure ═══════ -->
  <div class="arch-panel active" id="panel-plugin" role="tabpanel">
    <div class="panel-intro">
      <h3>Plugin Structure</h3>
      <p>VibeCrew is a <strong>Claude Code plugin</strong> &mdash; an add-on that gives Claude Code new abilities. When you open a project with VibeCrew installed, Claude Code reads a manifest file and automatically loads agents, skills, hooks, scripts, MCP servers, and permissions. Everything lives inside the <code>claude-plugin-vibe-crew/</code> directory.</p>
      <div class="key-points">
        <div class="key-point">
          <span class="key-icon purple">&#9632;</span>
          <div>
            <strong>What it is</strong>
            <p>A self-contained folder with a <code>plugin.json</code> manifest that registers agents, slash commands, hooks, scripts, and MCP servers &mdash; everything the system needs in one install.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="key-point">
          <span class="key-icon green">&#10003;</span>
          <div>
            <strong>What problem it solves</strong>
            <p>Without a plugin, you'd manually configure each agent, permission rule, and automation script for every project. The plugin bundles everything into a single install.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <MermaidDiagram chart={systemDiagram} caption="Plugin components and how Claude Code loads them" />
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════ Sub-Agents ═══════ -->
  <div class="arch-panel" id="panel-agents" role="tabpanel" hidden>
    <div class="panel-intro">
      <h3>Sub-Agents</h3>
      <p>Instead of one AI doing everything, VibeCrew splits work across <strong>specialized agents</strong> &mdash; like a real development team with planners, builders, testers, and reviewers. Most agents run on Opus, the most powerful model available. This is intentional: while Opus costs more per task and takes longer, the higher quality output means significantly less rework. Getting it right the first time saves more time and money than running a cheaper model that needs multiple correction rounds. Fast, mechanical tasks (routing, running shell commands) use Haiku, and template-driven output like documentation uses Sonnet.</p>
      <div class="key-points">
        <div class="key-point">
          <span class="key-icon purple">&#9632;</span>
          <div>
            <strong>What they are</strong>
            <p>AI specialists with scoped tool access, model selection, and turn budgets. Most run on Opus (the most capable model) because planning, coding, security, and analysis are tasks where mistakes are expensive to fix later. Haiku handles fast mechanical work, and Sonnet handles documentation.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="key-point">
          <span class="key-icon green">&#10003;</span>
          <div>
            <strong>What problem they solve</strong>
            <p>A single AI context window fills up fast when researching, coding, and testing in one session. Worktree agents offload expensive research into isolated contexts &mdash; only the distilled output enters your main session, saving thousands of tokens.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="agent-legend">
      <span class="legend-item"><span class="legend-dot" style="background:var(--color-accent)"></span> Opus (complex reasoning)</span>
      <span class="legend-item"><span class="legend-dot" style="background:var(--color-amber)"></span> Haiku (fast tasks)</span>
      <span class="legend-item"><span class="legend-dot" style="background:var(--color-blue)"></span> Sonnet (documentation)</span>
    </div>

    <MermaidDiagram chart={agentInteractionDiagram} caption="Agent topology: how agents hand off work to each other" />

    <h4 class="agent-table-heading">Agent Reference</h4>
    <div class="agent-table-wrap">
      <table class="agent-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Agent</th><th>Model</th><th>Isolation</th><th>What It Does</th><th>Triggered By</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Session Startup</strong></td>
            <td><span class="badge amber">Haiku</span></td>
            <td>Inline</td>
            <td>Environment check, state detection, handoff detection, routing</td>
            <td>Every session start</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Workflow Orchestrator</strong></td>
            <td><span class="badge opus">Opus</span></td>
            <td>Inline</td>
            <td>Routes between Tier 1 and Tier 2, coordinates agent handoffs, manages backlog</td>
            <td>Command routing</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Stack Scout</strong></td>
            <td><span class="badge opus">Opus</span></td>
            <td>Worktree</td>
            <td>Read-only research via WebSearch, Context7, Chrome DevTools. Produces TDR</td>
            <td>Tier 1 architecture step</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Builder</strong></td>
            <td><span class="badge opus">Opus</span></td>
            <td>Worktree</td>
            <td>Implements features: component selection, code generation, accessibility checks</td>
            <td>Tier 2 design + code phases</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Verifier</strong></td>
            <td><span class="badge amber">Haiku</span></td>
            <td>Inline</td>
            <td>Runs tests, lint, build, and type-check. Reports pass/fail results</td>
            <td><code>/check</code>, <code>/wrap</code>, <code>/run-backlog</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Performance Coach</strong></td>
            <td><span class="badge opus">Opus</span></td>
            <td>Inline</td>
            <td>Cross-session trend analysis, anti-pattern detection, CLAUDE.md mutation proposals</td>
            <td><code>/wrap</code> session end</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Code Auditor</strong></td>
            <td><span class="badge opus">Opus</span></td>
            <td>Worktree</td>
            <td>Read-only codebase analysis for existing project onboarding</td>
            <td><code>/onboard</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Security Auditor</strong></td>
            <td><span class="badge opus">Opus</span></td>
            <td>Worktree</td>
            <td>OWASP Top 10 security analysis: injection, auth, XSS, secrets</td>
            <td><code>/audit</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Doc Generator</strong></td>
            <td><span class="badge sonnet">Sonnet</span></td>
            <td>Inline</td>
            <td>Feature docs, CHANGELOG, release notes, VitePress sidebar regeneration</td>
            <td><code>/wrap</code>, Tier 2 docs phase</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Code Simplifier</strong></td>
            <td><span class="badge opus">Opus</span></td>
            <td>Worktree</td>
            <td>Dead code detection, abstraction flattening, API surface reduction</td>
            <td><code>/simplify</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>CI Healer</strong></td>
            <td><span class="badge opus">Opus</span></td>
            <td>Inline</td>
            <td>CI failure diagnosis and repair. Categorizes failures, max 3 fix attempts</td>
            <td><code>/heal</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Opponent Processor</strong></td>
            <td><span class="badge opus">Opus</span></td>
            <td>Worktree</td>
            <td>Devil's advocate for TDR decisions: counter-arguments, debate matrices, risks</td>
            <td>Tier 1 after TDR</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>Code Reviewer</strong></td>
            <td><span class="badge opus">Opus</span></td>
            <td>Worktree</td>
            <td>Read-only code review against feature spec, TDR compliance, conventions</td>
            <td><code>/review</code>, <code>/run-backlog</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><strong>System Reviewer</strong></td>
            <td><span class="badge opus">Opus</span></td>
            <td>Worktree</td>
            <td>Plugin meta-analysis, cross-project telemetry, ecosystem research</td>
            <td><code>/system-review</code></td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════ Hooks ═══════ -->
  <div class="arch-panel" id="panel-hooks" role="tabpanel" hidden>
    <div class="panel-intro">
      <h3>Hooks</h3>
      <p>Hooks are <strong>automatic safety checks</strong> that run behind the scenes at zero token cost. When an agent tries to write a file or run a command, hooks intercept the action and validate it before it executes. Think of them as security guards at every door &mdash; they check permissions instantly using small bash scripts, not AI processing.</p>
      <div class="key-points">
        <div class="key-point">
          <span class="key-icon purple">&#9632;</span>
          <div>
            <strong>What they are</strong>
            <p>Bash scripts triggered by Claude Code lifecycle events (session start, before/after tool use, session end). They read a JSON payload from stdin and return exit code 0 (allow) or 2 (block).</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="key-point">
          <span class="key-icon green">&#10003;</span>
          <div>
            <strong>What problem they solve</strong>
            <p>AI models can forget rules mid-session. Hooks enforce constraints deterministically &mdash; the phase gate blocks source code writes before the foundation is complete, the quality gate catches build errors, and the data protector blocks destructive commands. No token cost, no forgetting.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <MermaidDiagram chart={hooksDiagram} caption="Hook events mapped to their enforcement scripts" />
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════ Slash Commands ═══════ -->
  <div class="arch-panel" id="panel-commands" role="tabpanel" hidden>
    <div class="panel-intro">
      <h3>Slash Commands</h3>
      <p>Slash commands are the <strong>primary user interface</strong> to VibeCrew. You type a command starting with <code>/</code> and the system handles the rest. Each command is defined as a <code>SKILL.md</code> file with YAML frontmatter (name, description, arguments) and a markdown body containing the full workflow: preconditions, steps, error handling, and agent handoffs.</p>
      <div class="key-points">
        <div class="key-point">
          <span class="key-icon purple">&#9632;</span>
          <div>
            <strong>What they are</strong>
            <p>Commands across several categories: setup, planning, development, quality, safety, session management, and gamification. Some are script-only (zero token cost), others orchestrate multiple agents.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="key-point">
          <span class="key-icon green">&#10003;</span>
          <div>
            <strong>What problem they solve</strong>
            <p>Without slash commands, you'd need to manually prompt the AI with detailed instructions for every workflow step. Commands encode expert workflows into one-word triggers &mdash; <code>/wrap</code> runs quality checks, scores your session, generates docs, and commits, all automatically.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <MermaidDiagram chart={commandsDiagram} caption="How a slash command flows from user input to results" />
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════ Skills Reference ═══════ -->
  <div class="arch-panel" id="panel-skills" role="tabpanel" hidden>
    <div class="panel-intro">
      <h3>Skills Reference</h3>
      <p>Every slash command maps to a <strong>skill</strong> &mdash; a <code>SKILL.md</code> file in the <code>skills/</code> directory. Skills range from zero-cost script-only operations (like <code>/idea</code> and <code>/status</code>) to complex multi-agent orchestrations (like <code>/new-project</code> and <code>/wrap</code>). Use the category filters to browse all {commands.length} skills.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="skills-filters" id="skills-filters">
      <button class="filter-pill active" data-cat="all">All</button>
      {categories.map((cat) => (
        <button class="filter-pill" data-cat={cat}>{cat}</button>
      ))}
    </div>

    <div class="skills-table-wrap">
      <table class="agent-table" id="skills-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Command</th>
            <th>Category</th>
            <th>Agent(s)</th>
            <th>Tier</th>
            <th>Purpose</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {commands.map((cmd) => (
            <tr data-cat={cmd.category}>
              <td><code>{cmd.name}{cmd.args ? ` ${cmd.args}` : ''}</code></td>
              <td><span class="cat-badge">{cmd.category}</span></td>
              <td>{cmd.agent}</td>
              <td>{cmd.tier}</td>
              <td>{cmd.purpose}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════ Bash Scripts ═══════ -->
  <div class="arch-panel" id="panel-scripts" role="tabpanel" hidden>
    <div class="panel-intro">
      <h3>Bash Scripts</h3>
      <p>Scripts are the <strong>worker bees</strong> of VibeCrew &mdash; ~80 small bash programs that do the actual mechanical work. While agents think and make decisions, scripts create files, update project state, calculate scores, and send notifications. They run in milliseconds and always produce the same result for the same input.</p>
      <div class="key-points">
        <div class="key-point">
          <span class="key-icon purple">&#9632;</span>
          <div>
            <strong>What they are</strong>
            <p>Deterministic bash scripts organized by function: core lifecycle, feature management, quality scoring, project analysis, documentation, gamification, and state management. Agents never write state files directly &mdash; all mutations go through scripts.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="key-point">
          <span class="key-icon green">&#10003;</span>
          <div>
            <strong>What problem they solve</strong>
            <p>AI token cost scales with complexity. By offloading mechanical tasks (JSON manipulation, git operations, file formatting) to scripts, VibeCrew keeps AI focused on decisions while scripts handle bookkeeping at zero token cost. Scripts also guarantee consistency &mdash; state mutations are atomic and predictable.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <MermaidDiagram chart={scriptsDiagram} caption="Script categories and who calls them" />
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════ Runtime State ═══════ -->
  <div class="arch-panel" id="panel-runtime" role="tabpanel" hidden>
    <div class="panel-intro">
      <h3>Runtime State</h3>
      <p>Every project using VibeCrew gets a <code>.vibecrew/</code> folder that stores all runtime state: project configuration, foundation progress, feature backlog, session logs, Vibe Scores, architecture diagrams, and cross-session handoffs. This folder is the system's memory &mdash; it persists between sessions and lets agents pick up exactly where the last one left off.</p>
      <div class="key-points">
        <div class="key-point">
          <span class="key-icon purple">&#9632;</span>
          <div>
            <strong>What it is</strong>
            <p>A directory of JSON files and sub-folders created by <code>/setup</code>. Core files: <code>config.json</code> (preferences), <code>state.json</code> (current phase), <code>backlog.json</code> (feature specs), <code>gamification.json</code> (progression). Sub-directories hold sessions, scores, signals, locks, and architecture diagrams.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="key-point">
          <span class="key-icon green">&#10003;</span>
          <div>
            <strong>What problem it solves</strong>
            <p>AI sessions are stateless &mdash; each new conversation starts from scratch. The <code>.vibecrew/</code> folder gives the system persistent memory: which features are done, what phase you're in, your quality trends, and even architectural diagrams that agents reference instead of re-deriving structure from code.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <MermaidDiagram chart={runtimeStateDiagram} caption="Runtime state files and who reads/writes them" />
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════ Permissions & Safety ═══════ -->
  <div class="arch-panel" id="panel-safety" role="tabpanel" hidden>
    <div class="panel-intro">
      <h3>Permissions &amp; Safety</h3>
      <p>VibeCrew uses a <strong>defense-in-depth</strong> model with three layers of protection. Before any agent action reaches your filesystem, it passes through permission rules, hook guards, and quality gates. This means the AI can work productively without asking for permission every 5 seconds, while being physically unable to do anything destructive.</p>
      <div class="key-points">
        <div class="key-point">
          <span class="key-icon purple">&#9632;</span>
          <div>
            <strong>What it is</strong>
            <p>Three concentric safety layers: <code>settings.json</code> (allow and deny rules), PreToolUse hook scripts (phase gate, path restrictions, destructive command blocking), and Stop hook quality gates (typecheck, lint, build, cost limits).</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="key-point">
          <span class="key-icon green">&#10003;</span>
          <div>
            <strong>What problem it solves</strong>
            <p>AI agents can attempt risky actions &mdash; force-pushing, deleting files, running as root, or writing code before architecture is decided. VibeCrew blocks these deterministically with bash scripts, not by hoping the model remembers the rules.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="safety-columns">
      <div class="safety-card allowed">
        <h4>Allowed without prompts</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>File operations</strong> &mdash; Read, Write, Edit, Glob, Grep</li>
          <li><strong>npm scripts</strong> &mdash; test, lint, build, format, type-check</li>
          <li><strong>Git</strong> &mdash; status, log, diff, add, commit, checkout, branch, worktree, revert, tags</li>
          <li><strong>GitHub/GitLab CLI</strong> &mdash; PR create/list/view, pipeline view</li>
          <li><strong>Shell utilities</strong> &mdash; jq, cat, ls, wc, sort, head, tail, mkdir, cp, mv</li>
          <li><strong>Security audits</strong> &mdash; npm audit, pip audit, cargo audit, govulncheck</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="safety-card blocked">
        <h4>Always blocked</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Privilege escalation</strong> &mdash; <code>sudo</code>, <code>su</code>, <code>doas</code>, <code>pkexec</code></li>
          <li><strong>Force operations</strong> &mdash; <code>git push --force</code>, <code>git reset --hard</code>, <code>git clean -f</code></li>
          <li><strong>Destructive commands</strong> &mdash; <code>rm -rf /</code>, <code>rm -rf ~</code>, <code>chmod 777</code></li>
          <li><strong>System modifications</strong> &mdash; <code>npm install -g</code>, <code>kill -9</code>, <code>systemctl</code>, <code>crontab</code></li>
          <li><strong>Data destruction</strong> &mdash; <code>DROP TABLE</code>, <code>DELETE FROM</code> without WHERE (hook layer)</li>
          <li><strong>Path violations</strong> &mdash; writes outside project root, source code before foundation</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <MermaidDiagram chart={safetyDiagram} caption="Three-layer defense: permissions → hooks → quality gates" />
  </div>

  <!-- ═══════ Vibe Dashboard ═══════ -->
  <div class="arch-panel" id="panel-dashboard" role="tabpanel" hidden>
    <div class="panel-intro">
      <h3>Vibe Dashboard</h3>
      <p>Every VibeCrew project gets a <strong>browser-based dashboard</strong> scaffolded into its <code>docs/</code> folder during <code>/setup</code>. It's a VitePress-powered site that reads live data from <code>.vibecrew/</code> &mdash; the same JSON files that power the CLI &mdash; and renders it as interactive charts, Kanban boards, and settings editors. No database, no backend, just local JSON files.</p>
      <div class="key-points">
        <div class="key-point">
          <span class="key-icon purple">&#9632;</span>
          <div>
            <strong>What it is</strong>
            <p>A 7-tab VitePress site (Guide, Kanban Board, Session Stats, Trends, Coverage, Achievements, Settings) with VitePress data loaders that read <code>.vibecrew/*.json</code> files and render them as Vue components. Hot-reloads in dev mode when data changes.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="key-point">
          <span class="key-icon green">&#10003;</span>
          <div>
            <strong>What problem it solves</strong>
            <p>Terminal output is ephemeral &mdash; once you scroll past it, the data is gone. The dashboard gives you a persistent, visual interface to browse your backlog, track quality trends over time, review session history, and edit project settings without memorizing CLI commands.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <MermaidDiagram chart={dashboardDiagram} caption="Data flow from .vibecrew/ JSON files through VitePress to dashboard tabs" />

    <h4 class="agent-table-heading">Dashboard Tabs</h4>
    <div class="dashboard-grid">
      <div class="dash-card">
        <span class="dash-icon">&#128218;</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Guide</strong>
          <p>Getting-started docs, command reference, and workflow overview. The default landing tab.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="dash-card">
        <span class="dash-icon">&#128203;</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Kanban Board</strong>
          <p>7-column interactive board (Ideas &rarr; Done). Add ideas, drag cards between columns, launch Warp sessions. Reads <code>backlog.json</code>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="dash-card">
        <span class="dash-icon">&#128200;</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Session Stats</strong>
          <p>Total sessions, average Vibe Score, token usage, and estimated costs. Reads <code>sessions/*.json</code>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="dash-card">
        <span class="dash-icon">&#128201;</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Trends</strong>
          <p>Vibe Score trajectory, token breakdown, and agent activity over time. Reads <code>scores/*.json</code>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="dash-card">
        <span class="dash-icon">&#9989;</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Coverage</strong>
          <p>Test coverage gauge and feature delivery progress (completed vs. total backlog).</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="dash-card">
        <span class="dash-icon">&#127942;</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Achievements</strong>
          <p>Level, XP, badges, skill radar chart, and streak calendar. Reads <code>gamification.json</code>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="dash-card">
        <span class="dash-icon">&#9881;</span>
        <div>
          <strong>Settings</strong>
          <p>Browser-based editor for user profile, notifications, cost limits, MCP servers, and gamification toggles. Writes to <code>config.json</code>.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h4 class="agent-table-heading">CLI Equivalents</h4>
    <div class="agent-table-wrap">
      <table class="agent-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Dashboard Tab</th><th>CLI Command</th><th>What It Shows</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Kanban / Stats</td><td><code>/status</code></td><td>Feature backlog overview, session count, current feature</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Stats / Trends</td><td><code>/cost</code></td><td>Token usage, API costs, per-session breakdown</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Achievements</td><td><code>/achievements</code></td><td>Level, XP, badges, streaks, challenges</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Settings (profile)</td><td><code>/profile</code></td><td>Edit user profile dimensions interactively</td></tr>
